.Dd August 20, 2026
.Dt BLUED 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm blued
.Nd general-purpose Bluetooth Low Energy daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Bdv
.Op Fl a Ar adapter
.Op Fl c Ar configfile
.Op Fl f Ar bonds_file
.Op Fl L Ar logfile
.Fl s
.Nm
.Op Fl Bdrv
.Op Fl a Ar adapter
.Op Fl c Ar configfile
.Op Fl f Ar bonds_file
.Op Fl L Ar logfile
.Ar bdaddr
.Op Ar public | random
.Op Ar ...
.Nm
.Op Fl Bdv
.Op Fl a Ar adapter
.Op Fl c Ar configfile
.Op Fl f Ar bonds_file
.Op Fl L Ar logfile
.Fl p
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon is a general-purpose Bluetooth Low Energy daemon for
.Fx .
In its default central mode, it connects to BLE HID devices using the
HID over GATT Profile (HOGP) and injects HID reports into the kernel
via the vhid virtual HID transport.
It also supports a peripheral mode that advertises a GATT server.
.Pp
The kernel's native HID drivers
.Pq Xr hkbd 4 , hms 4 , hmt 4
automatically attach to the virtual device and process reports,
providing keyboard, mouse, and touchpad functionality.
.Pp
The
.Nm
daemon implements the BLE protocol stack from the ground up:
ATT, GATT, SMP (both Legacy and Secure Connections pairing),
and the HID over GATT Profile.
Multiple devices can be connected simultaneously by specifying
multiple
.Ar bdaddr
arguments.
.Pp
After establishing BLE connections and discovering services,
.Nm
enters a
.Xr capsicum 4
sandbox, restricting itself to only the file descriptors required
for operation.
.Pp
When launched by
.Xr serviced 8 ,
.Nm
is a named, on-demand service and is not launched as part of system boot.
The first managed client connection to
.Dq system.Bluetooth
activates the service and its
.Pa vhid.ko
kernel-module prerequisite.
.Pp
Once activated,
.Nm
validates the versioned bootstrap descriptor and activates every inherited
capability token before opening controllers, Bluetooth sockets, or device
nodes.
Failure to initialize the service channel or activate any token is fatal.
After initialization it selects the external capprotect shield itself;
.Xr serviced 8
retains shutdown authority through the process descriptor.
It registers the name
.Dq system.Bluetooth
and reports readiness only after initialization completes.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl a Ar adapter
Bluetooth adapter to use.
Default is
.Li ubt0 .
.It Fl B
Run as a daemon: fork into the background, log to
.Xr syslogd 8 ,
and write a PID file.
.It Fl c Ar configfile
Path to the configuration file.
Default is
.Pa /etc/blued.conf .
When managed by
.Xr serviced 8 ,
the default is
.Pa Config/blued.conf
below the selected
.Ev CAPABILITY_UNIT_DIR .
See
.Sx CONFIGURATION FILE
below.
.It Fl d
Enable debug output to stderr.
Equivalent to
.Fl v .
.It Fl f Ar bonds_file
Path to the bond storage file.
Default is
.Pa /var/db/blued/bonds .
.It Fl L Ar logfile
Log HCI packets to
.Ar logfile
in BTSnoop format.
The resulting file can be opened in Wireshark for analysis.
.It Fl p
Peripheral mode.
Advertise a GATT server with GAP, GATT, Device Information,
and custom services.
Accepts incoming BLE connections instead of initiating them.
.It Fl r
Enable auto-reconnect.
When the BLE link drops,
.Nm
will attempt to reconnect with exponential backoff
up to 60 seconds.
.It Fl s
Scan mode.
Scan for nearby BLE devices for 5 seconds and display results,
then exit.
Uses extended scanning on BT 5.0+ controllers.
.It Fl v
Increase verbosity.
Repeat for trace-level output
.Pq Fl vv .
.It Ar bdaddr
Bluetooth address of one or more remote HID devices.
Each address may optionally be followed by a
.Cm public
or
.Cm random
address type keyword.
The default address type is
.Cm public .
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION FILE
When started with the
.Fl c
option or via the
.Xr rc 8
system, the
.Nm
daemon reads a configuration file in UCL format.
The default path is
.Pa /etc/blued.conf .
A sample configuration file is installed to
.Pa /usr/share/examples/blued/blued.conf.sample .
.Pp
The file has the following top-level sections:
.Bl -tag -width "security"
.It Cm general
Controls logging verbosity
.Pq Cm loglevel ,
the bond database path
.Pq Cm bonddb ,
an optional BTSnoop packet log
.Pq Cm logfile ,
and the BLE peripheral advertised name
.Pq Cm peripheral_name , default Dq FreeBSD-BLE .
.It Cm adapters
A list of adapter names to use, or the string
.Dq auto
to discover all
.Li ubt*
adapters automatically.
.It Cm security
SMP pairing parameters:
.Cm io_capability
.Pq none, display_only, display_yesno, keyboard_only, keyboard_display ,
.Cm bondable
.Pq boolean ,
.Cm sc
.Pq off, on, or only
to select the Secure Connections policy, and
.Cm min_key_size
minimum encryption key size for legacy SMP pairing (7\(en16, default 16).
Secure Connections always requires 16 regardless of this setting.
.It Cm features
Feature toggles:
.Cm eatt
.Pq Enhanced ATT bearers, default true ,
.Cm privacy
.Pq RPA resolution ,
.Cm reconnect
.Pq auto-reconnect on link loss ,
.Cm reconnect_max_delay
in seconds,
.Cm rpa_timeout
RPA rotation timeout in seconds (1\(en3600, default 900),
.Cm peripheral_mode
and
.Cm scan_mode
.Pq booleans, require restart .
.It Cm devices
Optional per-device overrides keyed by BD_ADDR.
Each device block may specify
.Cm type
.Pq public or random
and
.Cm reconnect
.Pq boolean .
.It Cm service
Custom GATT service definitions for peripheral mode.
Each
.Cm service
block has a
.Cm uuid
(either
.Dq 0xNNNN
for 16-bit or a full 128-bit UUID string) and one or more
.Cm characteristic
sub-blocks.
Each characteristic specifies
.Cm uuid ,
.Cm properties
(comma-separated:
.Cm read , write , write_no_rsp , notify , indicate , auth_signed_write ) ,
.Cm permissions
(comma-separated:
.Cm read , write , read_encrypt , write_encrypt , read_authen , write_authen ) ,
and an optional
.Cm value
(hex string, e.g.,
.Dq 0100
for two bytes).
If properties include
.Cm notify
or
.Cm indicate ,
a Client Characteristic Configuration Descriptor (CCCD) is automatically added.
Up to 8 services with 8 characteristics each may be defined.
.El
.Sh CONTROL SOCKET
When running as a daemon,
.Nm
creates a
.Ux Ns -domain
control socket at
.Pa /var/run/blued.sock .
Clients first negotiate the versioned, length-prefixed binary protocol and
then exchange request-ID-correlated typed operations, replies, and events.
The public client interface is
.Xr libble 3 ;
.Xr bluedctl 8
provides the administrative command-line interface.
.Pp
The following commands are supported:
.Bl -tag -width "REMOVE_SERVICE"
.It Cm ADAPTERS
List available Bluetooth adapters and their state.
.It Cm ADD_CHAR Ar svc_handle Ar uuid Ar properties Ar permissions Op Ar value_hex
Add a characteristic to an existing service in the live GATT database.
The
.Ar svc_handle
is the handle returned by
.Cm ADD_SERVICE .
The
.Ar uuid
is either a 16-bit UUID
.Pq e.g., Li 0xFFE1
or a 128-bit UUID string.
.Ar properties
and
.Ar permissions
are comma-separated strings
.Pq e.g., Li read,notify .
If properties include
.Cm notify
or
.Cm indicate ,
a CCCD descriptor is automatically added.
Returns the characteristic value handle on success.
.It Cm ADD_INCLUDE Ar svc_handle Ar included_start Ar included_end Op Ar uuid16
Add an Included Service declaration to an existing service.
The include must be added before the service's first characteristic.
For included services with 16-bit UUIDs,
.Ar uuid16
may be omitted and is inferred from the included service declaration.
.It Cm ADD_SERVICE Ar uuid
Create a new GATT service in the live database.
The
.Ar uuid
is either a 16-bit UUID
.Pq e.g., Li 0xFFE0
or a 128-bit UUID string
.Pq e.g., Li 12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789abc .
Returns the service declaration handle on success.
After adding, the database hash is recomputed and a Service Changed
indication is sent to bonded clients that have enabled indications.
.It Cm BONDS
List all bonded devices with key information (LTK, IRK, Secure
Connections status, and device name).
.It Cm BOND_EXPORT
Export the bond database as text.
Each bonded device is listed with its address, type, key flags
(LTK, IRK, CSRK, Secure Connections, MITM), and device name.
.It Cm CONNECT Ar bdaddr Op Ar type
Initiate a connection to the specified device.
Currently only devices defined in the configuration file or on the
command line are fully supported for HOGP setup.
.It Cm CONNPARAMS Op Ar bdaddr
Display connection parameters (interval, latency, supervision timeout)
for all active connections, or for a specific device if
.Ar bdaddr
is provided.
The interval is shown in milliseconds (units of 1.25ms), latency
is the number of allowed skipped events, and timeout is in
milliseconds (units of 10ms).
.It Cm DISCONNECT Ar bdaddr
Disconnect a connected device.
.It Cm ECBFC_CONNECT Ar bdaddr Ar psm Ar count
Open Enhanced Credit Based Flow Control (ECBFC) channels to the
specified device.
The
.Ar psm
is the Protocol/Service Multiplexer and
.Ar count
is the number of channels to open (1\(en5).
.It Cm ECBFC_RECONFIG Ar bdaddr Ar mtu Ar mps
Reconfigure the MTU and MPS of existing EATT bearers to the
specified device.
The new
.Ar mtu
and
.Ar mps
must not be less than the current values (MTU can only increase).
.It Cm DISCOVER Ar bdaddr
Run GATT service, characteristic, and descriptor discovery on a
connected remote device.
Returns the full GATT hierarchy with UUIDs, handles, and characteristic
properties.
This command blocks while performing BLE I/O.
.It Cm HOGP_READ Ar bdaddr Ar report_id
Read a HID Feature Report from a connected HOGP device.
The
.Ar report_id
identifies which Feature Report to read.
Returns the report value as a hexadecimal byte string.
This command blocks while performing a single ATT Read transaction.
.It Cm HOGP_WRITE Ar bdaddr Ar report_id Ar hex
Write a HID Feature Report to a connected HOGP device.
The
.Ar report_id
identifies which Feature Report to write.
The
.Ar hex
argument is a hexadecimal byte string containing the report data.
This command blocks while performing a single ATT Write transaction.
.It Cm LIST
List all currently connected devices and their status.
.It Cm NUMCMP_REPLY Ar bdaddr Cm yes | no
Confirm or reject a Secure Connections numeric comparison during
SMP pairing.
The
.Ar bdaddr
identifies the device being paired.
.It Cm LOGLEVEL Op Ar level
Without an argument, returns the current log level.
With a
.Ar level
argument (0\(en5), sets the daemon log level at runtime.
Level 0 is errors only, 1 is informational, and 2+ enables trace output.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ echo LOGLEVEL | nc -U /var/run/blued.sock
OK LOGLEVEL 1

$ echo "LOGLEVEL 3" | nc -U /var/run/blued.sock
OK LOGLEVEL 3
.Ed
.It Cm PAIR Ar bdaddr
Check SMP bond status for a connected device.
.It Cm PASSKEY_REPLY Ar bdaddr Ar passkey
Provide a passkey for SMP pairing with the specified device.
The
.Ar passkey
is a 6-digit decimal number (000000\(en999999).
Pairing is performed automatically during connection setup: central mode
pairs on GATT authentication errors, and peripheral mode responds to
incoming pairing requests.
To re-pair a device, use
.Cm UNBOND
and reconnect.
.It Cm PHY
Show the current PHY (1M, 2M, or Coded) for all active connections.
.It Cm READ Ar bdaddr Ar handle
Read a characteristic value from a connected remote device.
The
.Ar handle
may be specified in hexadecimal with a
.Li 0x
prefix or in decimal.
Returns the value as a hexadecimal byte string.
This command blocks while performing a single ATT Read transaction.
.It Cm REMOVE_SERVICE Ar handle
Remove a service and all its attributes (characteristics, descriptors)
from the live GATT database.
The
.Ar handle
is the service declaration handle (as returned by
.Cm ADD_SERVICE
or shown by
.Cm SERVICES ) .
After removal, the database hash is recomputed and a Service Changed
indication is sent to bonded clients.
.It Cm SCAN
Start a BLE scan and return discovered devices.
.It Cm SERVICES
List all attributes in the local GATT database (peripheral mode).
.It Cm SET_VALUE Ar handle Ar hex
Set the value of a GATT attribute in the local database.
The
.Ar handle
may be specified in hexadecimal with a
.Li 0x
prefix or in decimal.
The
.Ar hex
argument is a hexadecimal byte string.
.It Cm SUBSCRIBE Ar bdaddr Ar handle
Subscribe to characteristic value change notifications from a
connected remote device by writing 0x0001 to the CCCD at
.Ar handle .
.It Cm UNSUBSCRIBE Ar bdaddr Ar handle
Unsubscribe from characteristic value change notifications by
writing 0x0000 to the CCCD at
.Ar handle .
.It Cm STATUS
Return daemon status including adapter and connection counts.
.It Cm UNBOND Ar bdaddr
Remove the bond for the specified device.
The device is also removed from the controller's filter accept list.
.It Cm WRITE Ar bdaddr Ar handle Ar hex
Write a value to a characteristic on a connected remote device.
The
.Ar handle
may be specified in hexadecimal with a
.Li 0x
prefix or in decimal.
The
.Ar hex
argument is a hexadecimal byte string (e.g.,
.Li 0100
for two bytes).
Uses the ATT Write Request procedure (expects a response).
This command blocks while performing a single ATT Write transaction.
.El
.Sh MULTI-ADAPTER
When the
.Cm adapters
configuration directive is set to
.Dq auto ,
.Nm
enumerates all
.Li ubt*
devices present at startup.
Each adapter is managed independently, and connections
are distributed across adapters as needed.
Specific adapters can be selected with the
.Fl a
flag or by listing them explicitly in the configuration file.
.Sh SECURITY
The
.Nm
daemon employs multiple layers of security:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Xr capsicum 4
capability mode is entered after initialization,
preventing the daemon from opening new file descriptors or
accessing the global namespace.
File descriptors passed to control socket clients are restricted with
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2
to
.Dv CAP_SEND , CAP_RECV , CAP_EVENT .
.It
A narrowly scoped descriptor broker is forked before capability mode.
It holds no daemon file descriptors and can only create L2CAP sequenced-packet
sockets, which it passes to the sandbox over a capability-limited local socket.
This permits bounded concurrent connects and reconnects without a finite
startup socket inventory.
.It
SMP pairing supports both LE Legacy Pairing and LE Secure Connections
(ECDH P-256).
Setting
.Cm sc
to
.Dq only
rejects legacy pairing entirely.
.El
.Sh PROTOCOL OVERVIEW
.Nm
implements the following Bluetooth LE protocol stack:
.Bl -bullet
.It
ATT (Attribute Protocol) \(em PDU exchange over L2CAP CID 0x0004
.It
GATT (Generic Attribute Profile) \(em service and characteristic discovery
.It
SMP (Security Manager Protocol) \(em LE Legacy and Secure Connections pairing
over L2CAP CID 0x0006
.It
HOGP (HID over GATT Profile) \(em HID Service discovery and report handling
.It
EATT (Enhanced ATT) \(em parallel GATT operations over L2CAP credit-based
channels (BT 5.1+)
.El
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width "SIGTERM"
.It Dv SIGHUP
Reload the configuration file.
The daemon re-reads the file specified by the
.Fl c
flag (or the default
.Pa /etc/blued.conf )
and applies runtime-changeable settings without restarting.
.Pp
The following settings are reloaded at runtime:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Cm loglevel
.It
.Cm reconnect
and
.Cm reconnect_max_delay
.It
.Cm rpa_timeout
.It
.Cm bondable , sc , io_capability , min_key_size
.It
.Cm privacy
.It
.Cm peripheral_name
.El
.Pp
The following settings require a full restart of
.Nm
to take effect:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Cm adapters
.It
.Cm peripheral_mode , scan_mode
.It
.Cm service
definitions (GATT database is built once at startup)
.It
.Cm pidfile , ctlsock , bonddb
paths
.El
.Pp
When a non-reloadable setting has changed, a warning is logged
indicating that a restart is required.
Active connections are not disrupted by a configuration reload.
.It Dv SIGTERM , SIGINT
Shut down the daemon gracefully.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/share/examples/blued/blued.conf.sample" -compact
.It Pa /Capabilities/System/Bluetooth.cap
Trusted system-service bundle used by
.Xr serviced 8 .
.It Pa /Capabilities/System/Bluetooth.cap/Units/blued.unit/Config/blued.conf
Base-system instance of the configuration installed with the managed unit;
versioned site bundles use their own selected unit directory.
.It Pa /usr/sbin/Bluetooth
Compatibility symlink to the executable in the system-service bundle.
.It Pa /etc/blued.conf
Default configuration file (UCL format).
.It Pa /var/run/blued.pid
PID file when running as a daemon.
.It Pa /var/run/blued.sock
.Ux Ns -domain
control socket.
.It Pa /var/db/blued/bonds
Standalone-mode bond storage database.
The managed unit instead receives its persistent state directory through the
.Dq storage:state
descriptor.
.It Pa /dev/vhid
Virtual HID control device.
.It Pa /dev/vhidN
Virtual HID instance devices.
.El
